// XFAIL: DF-232a a bare integer literal does not adopt the expected fixed-width
// type in PLAIN ASSIGNMENT position, and the mismatch is an internal compiler
// error rather than a diagnostic
//
// Found while writing SOS design 232 unit 1: `TIMERS[slot].deadline_ns = 0` on a
// `UInt64` field died with `cannot store i32 to i64*` on riscv32. Nothing about
// the kernel is involved — the same three lines reproduce on the host at any two
// widths.
//
// TWO DEFECTS IN ONE. The literal SHOULD adopt: an assignment target names an
// expected type exactly as an annotation does, and the language's rule is that a
// bare literal takes a fixed-width expectation wherever one is in force. And
// even where a type cannot be reconciled the compiler owes a clean refusal — an
// `internal compiler error` is what the design-192 fuzz oracle exists to catch,
// so the ICE is a finding on its own terms whatever is decided about adoption.
//
// THE MECHANISM, and the matrix it reaches (obligation 4, probed at every row):
// expected-type propagation into a bare literal is wired into the PLACE-LEND
// write paths and into the compound-assign RHS, and is simply absent from a
// plain `AssignStatement` whose target is a NAME or a FIELD PROJECTION. The
// typechecker never reconciles the two, so codegen reaches a raw store with
// mismatched LLVM types and dies there.
//
//   ADOPTS TODAY (unaffected):
//     let x: UInt32 = 0            annotation
//     Wide(b: 0)                   struct-literal field
//     var a: [UInt32; N] = [0; N]  repeat literal under an annotation
//     v += 1                       COMPOUND assignment
//     arr[0] = 5                   fixed-array element  (a place)
//     vec[0] = 5                   Vector element       (a place lend)
//
//   ICEs TODAY (this file):
//     v = 4                        a local of fixed width
//     w.b = 2                      a struct FIELD
//     rows[0].b = 7                a field THROUGH an element
//
// The split is exact and is why this is one bug rather than three: every row
// that works reaches the literal through a path that already carries an expected
// type, and every row that fails is the one statement form that does not.
//
// A suffixed literal works today (`0u32`), which is the spelling `timer_disarm`
// in sos/kernel/core/ uses in the meantime, with a comment pointing back here.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// local = 4
// field = 2
// elem field = 7

struct Wide { a: UInt64, b: UInt32 }

static N: Int = 2

func main() {
    var v: UInt32 = 9
    v = 4
    print("local = {v}")

    var w = Wide(a: 0, b: 0)
    w.b = 2
    print("field = {w.b}")

    var rows: [Wide; N] = [Wide(a: 0, b: 0); N]
    rows[0].b = 7
    print("elem field = {rows[0].b}")
}
